WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:10.370
Hey guys, Jared here.

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:14.300
Today we’re talking about an Amazon— and
she has nothing to do with endangered plant

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:16.200
species or free 2-day shipping.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:20.360
Wonder Woman wasn’t just the mega-box office
hit of the summer; the film and its eponymous

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:23.890
hero have become one of 2017’s biggest cultural
icons.

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:26.600
And, surprise, we have something to say about
it.

00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:32.140
Now, some have called Ares a pretty lackluster
villain, but his conflict with Diana is pretty

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:36.790
interesting compared to most superhero villains
who want to just rule the world, or rule the

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:39.740
world, or rule Asgard, or rule the universe.

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:43.920
And part of what makes the Greek God family
drama so cool is that Ares doesn’t just

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:45.760
blindly hate Diana.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:50.710
Their clash over the nature and fate of humanity
is, well, pretty philosophical.

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:54.500
So get ready to be lassoed by the truth in
this Wisecrack Edition on the Philosophy of

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:57.160
Wonder Woman aaaand cue the sirens.

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:03.420
One of the central conflicts of Wonder Woman
is, "do humans deserve to be saved?”

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:07.530
This makes sense since the titular character
is from an island of warrior women who exist

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:12.390
solely to save humans from destruction at
the hands of Ares, the God of War.

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:15.679
But as it turns out, when you’ve lived in
a tropical paradise with no boys allowed for

00:01:15.679 --> 00:01:19.840
a couple millennia, you might decide that
humans aren’t really worth the effort.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:22.830
“Zeus created man to be just, and wise,
strong and passionate…”

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:25.460
“That was a story, Diana!"

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:30.080
Much of Diana’s struggle is wrapped up in
the question of what it means to save people.

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:34.250
She wavers between a sense of duty, a sense
of caring, and a moment of Grade A apathy.

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:35.920
“They don't deserve our help, Steve.”

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:36.800
"It's...It's not

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:37.760
It's not about deserve!"

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:43.220
Meanwhile, Ares is fully committed to the
belief that humans suck a fat bag of popsicles

00:01:43.229 --> 00:01:47.810
and deserve not only abandonment but also
to die painfully via chemical weapons.

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:50.819
“Ares developed a weapon, the worst ever
devised.”

00:01:50.819 --> 00:01:54.819
Diana’s big hero statement of the film is
in response to Ares’ claim that.

00:01:54.819 --> 00:01:58.509
“They do not deserve your protection!”.

00:01:58.509 --> 00:02:00.880
She responds: “It’s not about deserve.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:02.520
It’s about what you believe.

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:07.160
And I believe in love.”

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:10.620
And we believe that that line is confusing
as f*ck.

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:14.680
But instead of dismissing it as bad writing,
in good ol’ Wisecrackian fashion, let’s

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:15.690
pull it apart.

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:20.349
But before we get to Diana, we have to understand
what she’s responding to— Ares’ belief

00:02:20.349 --> 00:02:23.690
that humans are worthless and can’t have
nice things.

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:29.079
Ares’ claim in the film has two parts: 1)
that humans deserve to be punished and 2)

00:02:29.079 --> 00:02:31.319
that they don’t deserve Diana’s help.

00:02:31.319 --> 00:02:33.569
“You were right, Diana.

00:02:33.569 --> 00:02:36.379
They don't deserve our help.

00:02:36.379 --> 00:02:38.640
They only deserve destruction.”

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:42.530
The first one is a question of something called
“retributive justice”— basically the

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:46.140
theory that argues that people who do bad things should go to jail.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:49.840
So what informs Ares’ retributive mission
to destroy humanity?

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:52.580
He believes that humans are, by nature, evil.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:03:03.960
“They always been and always will be weak,
cruel, selfish, and capable of the greatest horrors."

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:08.400
This is an essentialist view of human nature,
that humanity has inherent properties that

00:03:08.409 --> 00:03:09.870
make them what they are.

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:13.500
So, people are doomed to be useless assholes
forever.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:15.560
So it makes sense why you’d want to
get rid of them.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:19.860
Ares claim is bolstered by the sum of humanity’s
actions over the last couple thousand years.

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:22.269
In that time, humans have done a lot.

00:03:22.269 --> 00:03:28.489
They genocided a bunch of people, destroyed
the environment, and made this Logan Paul guy famous…

00:03:28.489 --> 00:03:32.319
“Mankind stole this world from us.

00:03:32.319 --> 00:03:35.690
They ruined it, day by day…”

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:38.090
So that’s the first part of Ares’ claim.

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:42.709
Now for the second one; one that is much more
central to Diana and the film’s arc— the

00:03:42.709 --> 00:03:46.999
question of who deserves to be saved, even
if it is from their own kind.

00:03:46.999 --> 00:03:48.909
This is “distributive justice.”

00:03:48.909 --> 00:03:54.420
In Ares’ calculation, being saved from death
is not something that one is entitled to— it

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:58.200
is something that is distributed based on
merit, as shown by the actions of the individual.

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:01.500
Or in this case, the actions of the group,
spanning three-plus millennia.

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:04.879
This seems to be a pretty common idea among
immortals.

00:04:04.879 --> 00:04:08.579
Diana’s mother, Hippolyta, tells her, "They
do not deserve you.”

00:04:08.579 --> 00:04:12.799
This all reflects one popular approach to
distributive justice, called desert theory

00:04:12.799 --> 00:04:15.909
which argues
that people deserve things— jobs, money,

00:04:15.909 --> 00:04:20.320
awards — based on their actions rather than
on their needs or the benefit it would bring

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:21.320
to society.

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:25.509
Now, although Diana stays generally consistent
in her desire to help humanity, the logic

00:04:25.509 --> 00:04:28.610
behind this choice develops and changes throughout
the film.

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:32.889
And while we see people justifying their actions
with distributive or retributive frameworks,

00:04:32.889 --> 00:04:35.050
Wonder Woman goes a different route.

00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:39.599
Diana is raised to believe that the Amazons
have a sacred duty to stop Ares and defend

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:40.600
the world.

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:43.500
So when she sets out for Europe, she’s not
doing it because she believes that humans

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:44.599
deserve anything.

00:04:44.599 --> 00:04:48.100
It’s because she believes she has a moral
obligation to help.

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:51.919
This moral obligation carries her through
most of the film, even as she watches British

00:04:51.919 --> 00:04:55.320
generals send soldiers to their deaths "You
should be ashamed" and Germans

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:57.090
taking villagers as slaves.

00:04:57.090 --> 00:05:02.100
But at some point, she realizes that people
suck, and it wasn’t totally Ares’ fault

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:03.750
that they were bayoneting each other.

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:05.039
“Ares is dead.

00:05:05.039 --> 00:05:06.819
They can now stop fighting now.

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:07.820
Why are they still fighting?”

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:12.600
Since her duty was to defend the world from
Ares and not from sh*tty humans acting on

00:05:12.610 --> 00:05:16.620
their own accord, she’s not really sure
where her moral obligations lie anymore.

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:20.910
All of a sudden, her mom’s wise words sound
a whole lot wiser.

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:21.940
“My mother was right.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:24.080
She said the world of men do not deserve you.

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:26.800
Diana is faced with a crisis of conscience.

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:31.539
She is no longer bound by duty, and her knowledge
of human atrocities lead her to the conclusion

00:05:31.539 --> 00:05:35.539
that humans do not merit her help as a matter
of justice either.

00:05:35.539 --> 00:05:40.000
Although Diana makes it less obvious than
Ares does, she is also motivated by a kind

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:41.000
of essentialism.

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:43.360
Let’s call this the “Battle of Essentialisms.”

00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:48.210
Ares says human are inherently evil, Diana
says they’reinherently good.

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:53.110
While Ares never abandons his essentialism,
Diana does when she discovers humans can be

00:05:53.110 --> 00:05:55.419
dickbags all on their own.

00:05:55.419 --> 00:05:59.650
We don’t see too much more of this philosophical
crisis, but all is resolved by the time she

00:05:59.650 --> 00:06:01.699
says, “I believe in love.”

00:06:01.699 --> 00:06:05.620
At first it might sound like she believes
in the love of Steve Trevor, but that doesn’t

00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:08.490
make a ton of sense, and let’s give her
a little more credit than that.

00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:13.270
It’s only at the end that it becomes clear
to her— and to us— why saving people is

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:14.810
right thing to do.

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:19.300
What differentiates Diana at the end from
someone like Captain America is that she realizes

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:21.699
that it’s not her duty to help people.

00:06:21.699 --> 00:06:25.639
She just wants to because she loves the shit
out of humanity.

00:06:25.639 --> 00:06:26.830
"Baby!!"

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:30.990
Even though that might sound like a saccharine,
Care Bear cop-out, it isn’t.

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:35.879
While she leaves behind the ideas of duty
and deserving, Wonder Woman taps into a totally

00:06:35.879 --> 00:06:41.350
different ethical approach that’s pretty
unique in the superhero genre- care ethics.

00:06:41.350 --> 00:06:45.539
Care ethics focuses on the relationship between
the person who is caring for someone and the

00:06:45.539 --> 00:06:46.699
person who is cared-for.

00:06:46.699 --> 00:06:53.100
A good example: a parent who loves their child,
even if that child is terrible, or Logan Paul.

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:57.600
Unlike most traditional approaches to ethics,
care ethics rejects universalized principles

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:02.659
for how to make judgments or how to act—
everything is contextualized within the given

00:07:02.659 --> 00:07:03.659
relationship.

00:07:03.659 --> 00:07:07.060
However, certain practices are pretty important
like: respect, empathy, and attention to the

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:08.150
needs of others.

00:07:08.150 --> 00:07:11.530
Sometimes these relationships are on equal
footing, but not always.

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:16.470
Wonder Woman is not our equal, so it’s
more like a person caring for a perfect puppy.

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:20.629
Just like you’re not born with an obligation
to care for a dog, Wonder Woman knowingly

00:07:20.629 --> 00:07:25.610
chooses to enter a relationship of care with
people who will never be able to entirely

00:07:25.610 --> 00:07:28.120
reciprocate, and maybe don’t deserve it.

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:32.680
She isn’t pursuing a defined goal, other
than making the world a better place, or holding

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:37.580
firm to certain universal principles like
Batman’s idea that killing is always wrong

00:07:37.580 --> 00:07:42.740
or Captain America’s belief in The American
Way as long as you forget that whole hail Hydra thing-- she is

00:07:42.740 --> 00:07:46.539
responding, as needed, to the needs of those
she cares for.

00:07:46.539 --> 00:07:51.439
At the end of the film, Diana’s relationship
rejects a kind of distributive or retributive

00:07:51.439 --> 00:07:54.370
justice, and instead makes a choice to love
them.

00:07:54.370 --> 00:08:00.740
“But then I glimpsed the darkness that lives
within their light and learned that inside

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:04.600
every one of them there will always be both.

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:11.800
And now I know that only love can truly save
the world."

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:14.800
Diana’s belief in love isn’t a meaningless
platitude.

00:08:14.810 --> 00:08:19.130
It’s a declaration of her commitment to
a particular state of mind and action that

00:08:19.130 --> 00:08:21.520
guides her decisions as a hero.

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:26.169
This is clear in her decision not to kill
Dr. Poison, despite her being a war criminal

00:08:26.169 --> 00:08:27.820
and also really creepy.

00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:36.100
“She is the perfect example of these humans
and unworthy of your sympathy in every way.”

00:08:36.100 --> 00:08:39.460
Although it would be a bit of a stretch to
say that Wonder Woman enters a relationship

00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:43.510
of care with Colonel Mustard Gas just because
she decides not to throw a literal tank at

00:08:43.510 --> 00:08:46.780
her, it’s indicative of Diana’s change
in mindset.

00:08:46.780 --> 00:08:51.210
This is visually paralleled by Dr. Poison’s
mask flying off piece by piece.

00:08:51.210 --> 00:08:56.380
As the wind uncovers her true, scarred, ugly
self, there is a very obvious metaphor, and

00:08:56.380 --> 00:09:00.520
Diana recognizes the complex nature and individual
differences of humans.

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:04.730
“They are everything you say, but so much
more.”

00:09:04.730 --> 00:09:09.660
Even though, by most calculations, Dr. Poison
probably deserves to die, Diana rejects the

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:14.690
concept of deserving, and by extension rejects
the Desert Theory justice that Ares is so

00:09:14.690 --> 00:09:16.030
fond of.

00:09:16.030 --> 00:09:21.320
Every superhero has his or her or its reasons
for blowing stuff up and saving the day.

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:25.510
Wonder Woman defines Diana’s ethos of care
and solidifies her role in the Justice League

00:09:25.510 --> 00:09:27.750
for many franchises to come.

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:32.690
But it also draws out a frequent theme in
comics: the worthiness of the people saved.

00:09:32.690 --> 00:09:36.640
Gotham doesn’t deserve Batman, and America
doesn’t deserve Steve Rogers.

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:40.680
Wonder Woman raises a question that we don’t
usually hear: does humanity deserve to be

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:42.050
saved at all?

00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:45.290
And even if they don’t, should we save them
anyway?

00:09:45.290 --> 00:09:46.840
Thanks for watching you guys, peace.

